WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.252
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You're
going through migrating

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:04.740
your monolithic application
to microservices on GKE,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.270
but you've hit a roadblock.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.540
There are so many features and
services in your application,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.660
and you don't know which ones
you should migrate first.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.060
Stay tuned to find out
more in today's episode

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.680
of "Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:27.710
CARTER MORGAN: When you
plan your migration,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.240
it's tempting to
start with features

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.920
that are trivial to migrate.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.750
This might be a quick
win, but might not

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.600
benefit your project the
most over the long term.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.860
Instead of diving straight
into the migration,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.890
you should spend time
evaluating each feature,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.390
and create a plan
for its migration.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.057
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And to do so, there's

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:44.890
a list of ingredients
that can help.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.240
Start with a business
process evaluation.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.550
Don't forget that this will
be a learning experience,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.690
and there will be
mistakes to learn from.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.420
So for that reason,
the initial efforts

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.940
should not involve
business critical systems,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.370
but they should still represent
a significant use case

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.082
for the team to learn from.

00:01:00.082 --> 00:01:02.540
CARTER MORGAN: From a design
and development point of view,

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.220
you want to pick features
that have the least

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.720
number of dependencies
and which are

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.820
easy to refactor if necessary.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.730
And also consider the costs
and complexity of developing

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.358
or refactoring as needed.

00:01:12.358 --> 00:01:14.400
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: From
operations point of view,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.000
think about features that
can afford the downtime

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.320
of the cut-over window.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.140
If you need to
minimize downtime,

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.640
migrating features that
require high availability

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.320
can mean extra work.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.880
CARTER MORGAN: And
finally, choose

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.920
teams that have
well-defined processes

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.840
to lead the initial
efforts, because you

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.030
want them to lead the way
for this migration journey

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:33.800
and understand that
they will encounter

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.915
new challenges for which
they must find solutions.

00:01:36.915 --> 00:01:39.290
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Taking all
these factors into account,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.110
this recipe calls for
divide and conquer approach,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.788
where we find challenging
but meaningful features.

00:01:44.788 --> 00:01:47.330
CARTER MORGAN: The ideal feature
requires little refactoring,

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.140
is stateless, has no
external data requirements,

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.695
and has few or no dependencies.

00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:53.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Let's
consider our friends

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.970
at the ice cream theory
and their migration.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.000
Since they're thinking about a
full migration of their website

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.330
to microservices, a good
migration order for them

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.330
could be that they migrate
the front end first, then

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.730
think about the
stateless features.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.550
Next could be the features
with independent data sets,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.480
such as services that list
their brick and mortar stores.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.510
And then finally, come to
features with shared data sets,

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.108
like the business logic
of the platform itself.

00:02:20.108 --> 00:02:21.650
CARTER MORGAN: The
platform front end

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.300
followed by any
stateless features,

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.490
like a currency
conversion system,

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.950
go first, because they
usually have few dependents

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.140
and often require
limited refactoring.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.620
Because in the initial
migration recipe,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.090
the back end API is
still being served

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.480
from the legacy data center
or runtime hosted on premises

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.947
or on another cloud.

00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:41.780
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: After
stateless features,

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.360
components whose data sets have
no dependencies on other data

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.790
sets, such as services to list
the brick and mortar store,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.660
are the next to migrate.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.570
These are easier to extract
and move to the modern storage

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:57.050
system, like Cloud storage or
Cloud Firestore for managed

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:00.470
data storage, Cloud SQL
for relational data,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.280
or Cloud Firestore or Datastore
for the document based no SQL

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.670
data.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.810
CARTER MORGAN: Finally,
features with shared data

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.203
sets, like the business logic
of the e-commerce platform,

00:03:11.203 --> 00:03:12.620
are the hardest
to migrate because

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.170
of the requirements for
consistency, distribution,

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.980
access, and latency across
legacy and new services.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.110
Since data migration
is not as simple,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:24.380
we are dedicating the
entire next episode to it.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.448
Stay tuned.

00:03:25.448 --> 00:03:27.240
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, there you have it.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.110
If you are looking to migrate
a monolithic application

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.630
to microservices, then
you've got a recipe

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.450
to decide which features
to migrate first.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.430
Stay tuned for the next episode,
where we will learn about--

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.305
CARTER MORGAN: The data
migration strategies.

00:03:40.305 --> 00:03:41.930
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That's all for today

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.130
on "Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.755
CARTER MORGAN: If you would like
